(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-axial-skeleton-quiz"]={"title":"The Axial Skeleton — Anatomy Exam 4","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What are the two types of bone, based on the relative amount of solid matter and space they contain?","choices":["Compact bone and spongy (trabecular/cancellous) bone","Primary bone and secondary ossification bone","Periosteum tissue and perichondrium tissue","Long bone shape and short bone shape"],"answer":0,"exp":"The two types of bone are compact bone and spongy (also called trabecular or cancellous) bone, differing in their relative amount of solid matter versus space. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the periosteum?","choices":["The fibrous connective tissue covering that surrounds bone","The membrane lining the medullary cavity","The tissue surrounding cartilage, excluding articular cartilage","The layer of compact bone beneath spongy bone"],"answer":0,"exp":"The periosteum is the fibrous connective tissue covering that surrounds bone. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which classification of bone is tubular in structure, such as the humerus and phalanges?","choices":["Short bones","Flat bones","Sesamoid bones","Long bones"],"answer":3,"exp":"Long bones are tubular structures, with the humerus and phalanges given as examples. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is a sesamoid bone, and what is an example given in the material?","choices":["A bone that develops within certain tendons to protect them from wear; the patella is an example","A tubular bone with a shaft and two ends; the humerus is the example given","A protective flat bone forming a broad plate; the cranium is the example given","A bone found exclusively within the face; the vomer is the example given"],"answer":0,"exp":"A sesamoid bone develops within certain tendons and protects them from excessive wear and tear; the patella is the example given. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Where is the primary ossification center located in a long bone, and what is its function?","choices":["Between the diaphysis and epiphysis; it is the main site of longitudinal bone growth in children","In the diaphysis; it is the first area where bone tissue replaces cartilage during fetal development","At the periosteum; it lays down new bone tissue specifically during fracture healing and repair","In the epiphyses; it appears sometime after birth to further contribute to bone growth"],"answer":1,"exp":"The primary ossification center is located in the diaphysis (shaft) of the long bone and is the first area where bone tissue replaces the cartilage model during fetal development. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Where are the secondary ossification centers located, and when do they appear?","choices":["In the diaphysis; they appear before birth","In the epiphyses; they appear after birth","In the epiphyseal plate; they appear continuously throughout adulthood","At the periosteum; they appear during fracture healing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Secondary ossification centers are located in the epiphyses (ends) of the long bone and appear after birth, contributing to growth of the bone ends. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is the epiphyseal plate (growth plate), and where is it located?","choices":["Hyaline cartilage between the diaphysis and epiphysis; the main site of longitudinal growth","A cavity located within the diaphysis that contains yellow bone marrow","Dense compact bone tissue found specifically at both articulating ends of long bones","A fibrous connective tissue covering that surrounds the entire outer surface of the bone"],"answer":0,"exp":"The epiphyseal plate (growth plate) is made of hyaline cartilage located between the diaphysis and epiphysis, serving as the main site of longitudinal growth in children and adolescents. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What bones does the axial skeleton consist of?","choices":["Only the vertebral column by itself, without the skull or thoracic cage","The bones of the limbs, the pectoral girdle, and the pelvic girdle","Bones of the head, neck (cervical vertebrae), and trunk (ribs, sternum, vertebrae, sacrum)","Only the skull and the mandible, without any neck or trunk bones"],"answer":2,"exp":"The axial skeleton consists of the bones of the head, neck (cervical vertebrae), and trunk (ribs, sternum, vertebrae, sacrum). — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as a function of the axial skeleton?","choices":["Absorbing shock and jolts specifically while walking, running, or jumping","Moving the upper and lower limbs through their full range of motion","Providing a framework that supports and protects organs in the dorsal and ventral body cavities","Forming the acetabulum, the hip socket that receives the head of the femur"],"answer":2,"exp":"The axial skeleton provides a framework that supports and protects organs in the dorsal and ventral body cavities, in addition to protecting special sense organs and anchoring posture/respiration muscles. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is the lambdoid suture?","choices":["The suture between the temporal bone and the parietal bone","The suture between the two parietal bones","The suture between the occipital bone and the two parietal bones","The suture between the frontal bone and the two parietal bones"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lambdoid suture is the suture between the occipital bone and the two parietal bones. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What large opening in the occipital bone allows passage of the spinal cord?","choices":["Condylar fossa","Hypoglossal canal","Foramen magnum","Jugular foramen"],"answer":2,"exp":"The foramen magnum is the large opening in the occipital bone through which the spinal cord passes. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which paired occipital landmarks articulate with the atlas (C1) vertebra?","choices":["Occipital condyles","Superior nuchal lines","External occipital protuberance","Hypoglossal canals"],"answer":0,"exp":"The occipital condyles are the paired landmarks that articulate with the atlas (C1) vertebra. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the frontal crest, and where is it found?","choices":["An external landmark near the supra-orbital margins","An internal landmark of the frontal bone","A suture between the frontal and nasal bones","A foramen for the supra-orbital nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The frontal crest is an internal-view landmark of the frontal bone, alongside the frontal sinuses. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What passes through the supra-orbital foramen?","choices":["The oculomotor nerve, cranial nerve three (CN III), alone","The facial nerve, cranial nerve seven (CN VII), exclusively","The optic nerve, cranial nerve two (CN II), by itself","The supra-orbital nerve (a branch of CN V) and supra-orbital artery"],"answer":3,"exp":"The supra-orbital nerve, a branch of the trigeminal nerve (CN V), and the supra-orbital artery pass through the supra-orbital foramen. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which temporal bone landmark contains the tympanic cavity and auditory ossicles internally?","choices":["Squamous part","Mastoid process","Petrous part","Zygomatic process"],"answer":2,"exp":"The petrous part of the temporal bone, viewed internally, contains the tympanic cavity and auditory ossicles. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What passes through the stylomastoid foramen?","choices":["Air conducting sound to the eardrum, the internal carotid artery, and the facial nerve","The optic nerve alone, exclusively, with no other named structures","The oculomotor nerve alone, controlling extraocular eye movement","The spinal cord alone, as it descends from the brainstem"],"answer":0,"exp":"Per the material, structures using the stylomastoid foramen/external acoustic meatus/carotid canal region include air that conducts sound to the eardrum, the internal carotid artery, and the facial nerve. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is the sella turcica, and what does it house?","choices":["A saddle-shaped depression on the sphenoid body that houses the pituitary gland (hypophyseal fossa)","A ridge on the ethmoid bone that anchors the nasal septum superiorly","A bony process of the temporal bone that helps form the zygomatic arch","A paired foramen in the sphenoid transmitting branches of the trigeminal nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"The sella turcica is a saddle-shaped depression on the body of the sphenoid, containing the hypophyseal fossa that houses the pituitary gland. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which two openings in the sphenoid transmit branches of the trigeminal nerve (mandibular division) and other structures near the base of the skull?","choices":["Foramen ovale and foramen spinosum","Foramen magnum and jugular foramen","Cribriform plate foramina","Optic canal and superior orbital fissure"],"answer":0,"exp":"The foramen ovale and foramen spinosum are openings in the sphenoid, with the foramen ovale transmitting the mandibular nerve (V3). — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is the crista galli, and where is it located?","choices":["A small foramen within the ethmoid bone that specifically transmits the optic nerve toward the orbit","A lateral projection formed by the sphenoid bone's greater wing, near the orbit","A ridge located along the posterior surface of the occipital bone, near the foramen magnum","A midline projection of the ethmoid bone, serving as an attachment point for the falx cerebri"],"answer":3,"exp":"The crista galli is a midline projection of the ethmoid bone that serves as an attachment point for the falx cerebri (a dural fold). — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What structure of the ethmoid is perforated by small foramina that transmit olfactory nerve fibers?","choices":["Superior nasal concha","Ethmoidal labyrinth","Perpendicular plate","Cribriform plate"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cribriform plate is perforated by small foramina that transmit olfactory nerve fibers from the nasal cavity to the brain. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What are the three cranial fossae?","choices":["Medial, lateral, and posterior cranial fossa","Anterior, middle, and posterior cranial fossa","Superior, middle, and inferior cranial fossa","Anterior, lateral, and posterior cranial fossa"],"answer":1,"exp":"The three cranial fossae are the anterior cranial fossa, middle cranial fossa, and posterior cranial fossa. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which maxillary landmark contains the air-filled sinuses of the upper jaw?","choices":["Maxillary sinuses","Palatine processes","Alveolar processes","Infra-orbital groove"],"answer":0,"exp":"The maxillary sinuses are the air-filled chambers within the maxillae. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What does the vomer form?","choices":["The zygomatic arch along the lateral aspect of the skull","The inferior portion of the bony nasal septum","A portion of the bony roof of the mouth","Part of the lateral wall of the bony orbit"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vomer forms the inferior portion of the bony nasal septum. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which mandibular landmark is the site of the temporomandibular joint articulation?","choices":["Mental foramen opening","Coronoid process apex","Angle of the mandible","Condylar process (head)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The condylar process, specifically its head, articulates with the mandibular fossa of the temporal bone at the temporomandibular joint. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What passes through the mandibular foramen and mandibular canal?","choices":["The internal carotid artery, running through the base of the skull","The optic nerve, traveling from the eye toward the brain","Structures supplying the lower teeth, exiting anteriorly at the mental foramen","The facial nerve exclusively, without any accompanying blood vessels"],"answer":2,"exp":"The mandibular foramen and canal transmit the neurovascular structures supplying the lower teeth, which exit anteriorly at the mental foramen as the mental nerve/vessels. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which bone forms the roof of the orbit?","choices":["Maxilla","Zygomatic bone","Sphenoid","Frontal bone"],"answer":3,"exp":"The frontal bone forms the roof of the orbit. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which bone forms the floor of the orbit?","choices":["Frontal bone","Zygomatic bone","Maxilla","Sphenoid"],"answer":2,"exp":"The maxilla forms the floor of the orbit. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What structures make up the nasal septum?","choices":["The sphenoid bone together with the frontal bone","The vomer and the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid","The paired nasal bones and the lacrimal bones","The maxilla bone together with the palatine bone"],"answer":1,"exp":"The nasal septum is made up of the vomer (inferiorly) and the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid (superiorly). — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Does the hyoid bone articulate directly with any other bone?","choices":["Yes — it articulates directly with the styloid process of the adjacent temporal bone","Yes — it articulates directly with the thyroid cartilage of the larynx below it","No — it is suspended by ligaments and does not articulate with any bones, making it extremely mobile","Yes — it articulates directly with the posterior body of the mandible above it"],"answer":2,"exp":"The hyoid bone does not articulate with any other bones; it is suspended by the stylohyoid ligaments, which makes it extremely mobile. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What are fontanelles, and what is their function?","choices":["Membranous areas where sutures will eventually form, allowing distortion of the skull during childbirth","Small foramina present throughout the infant skull that transmit individual cranial nerves to the scalp","Fully ossified adult joints of the skull that form only well after skeletal maturity is reached","Permanent cartilage plates within the skull that are never expected to undergo ossification"],"answer":0,"exp":"Fontanelles are membranous areas of the infant skull where sutures will eventually form; they allow the skull to distort somewhat during childbirth. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 58"}]};
